                  "explanation": "Sacks's objection was not to accuracy but to incompleteness. The modern case history correctly names the disorder yet tells us nothing about the 'who' — the suffering, adapting individual. He called for deepening the case into a narrative that restores the human subject. The other options misstate his complaint: he valued accurate diagnosis and never argued against imaging; the problem was too LITTLE about the person, not too much.",
